::STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Which measure is most affected by an extreme outlier?
{
=A. Mean#Correct. The mean uses every value, so an extreme value can pull it strongly.
~B. Median#The median is resistant because it depends on order, not every distance.
~C. Mode#The mode is the most frequent value and may not change because of one outlier.
~D. IQR#IQR focuses on the middle half and is less affected by extremes.
}

::STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
A data set is strongly right-skewed. Which center is usually better for a typical value?
{
~A. Mean#The mean is pulled toward the long right tail.
=B. Median#Correct. The median is more resistant to skew and outliers.
~C. Maximum#The maximum is an extreme value, not a center.
~D. Range#Range measures spread, not center.
}

::STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.6
Question:
A roughly normal model has mean 50 and standard deviation 4. About what percent is between 46 and 54?
{
~A. About 95%#About 95% is within two standard deviations.
~B. About 16%#About 16% is one tail beyond one standard deviation.
=C. About 68%#Correct. 46 to 54 is within one standard deviation of the mean.
~D. About 99.7%#About 99.7% is within three standard deviations.
}

::STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Which data shape is least appropriate for one normal model?
{
~A. One mound-shaped symmetric cluster#This is the general shape expected for a normal model.
~B. Roughly balanced data around one center#Balanced data around one center may support a normal model.
~C. No strong outliers and one peak#No strong outliers and one peak supports normal model use.
=D. Two separate clusters#Correct. Two clusters suggest more than one group or process.
}

::STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
A normal model has mean 80 and standard deviation 10. About what percent is above 90?
{
=A. About 16%#Correct. 90 is one standard deviation above the mean, so the upper tail is about 16%.
~B. About 34%#About 34% is between the mean and one standard deviation above.
~C. About 68%#About 68% is within one standard deviation.
~D. About 84%#About 84% is below one standard deviation above the mean.
}

